course: dictionary
lesson: 6606
title: Nonsense: silly words and silly ideas
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Nonsense is a word you will meet in stories, poems and everyday talk. Read the words around it to find its meaning. «Scary predictions online are usually nonsense meant to get attention.» Predictions that are made up to get attention are probably not true, so nonsense means silly or untrue ideas.
explain: Nonsense can also be a shout. When Alice says «Nonsense!» to the Queen, she is telling her clearly that she disagrees. Ask yourself: is someone saying something silly, or are they disagreeing? The sentence around the word will show you which.
explain: Some nonsense is made on purpose, like the made-up words blim and zort, or a nonsense poem. These are written for fun and do not have real meanings. Try the test: if the words are made up to be silly, nonsense means they have no real meaning.
explain: Nonsense is a noun you cannot count, so say «some nonsense», not «a nonsense». Phrases like «talk nonsense», «utter nonsense» and «stuff and nonsense» all keep the same meaning: silly, untrue or meaningless.
fact: Nonsense means words or ideas that are silly, untrue or have no meaning.
fact: Nonsense can also mean silly or annoying behaviour, as in «Stop this nonsense!»
fact: «Nonsense!» is said to show that you strongly disagree with something.
fact: Nonsense words, like blim or zort, and nonsense poems are made up for fun.
fact: Useful phrases: «talk nonsense», «utter nonsense», «stuff and nonsense», and «make nonsense of» something, which means to show it is silly or untrue.
exercise: «Scary predictions online are usually nonsense meant to get attention.» What does nonsense mean here? | A kind of online game | Very scary warnings | Helpful facts from experts | *Silly, untrue ideas
exercise: Alice says «Nonsense!» to the Queen. What is she doing? | *Telling the Queen she strongly disagrees | Asking for a drink | Falling asleep | Clapping her hands
exercise: Which of these is a nonsense word, made up for fun? | table | jumped | happy | *zort
test: «Stop this nonsense!» What does nonsense mean in this sentence? | A gift for a friend | A big, quiet room | *Silly or annoying behaviour | Clear and helpful advice
test: Which phrase means talking silly things? | *talk nonsense | eat your dinner | close the door | read a book
exam: The phrase «the bee's knees» meant something excellent in 1920s slang, so why is it called playful nonsense? | It was the Queen's own order | *Its words were silly and fun, not meant to be taken literally | It was said only in science lessons | It was a real fact about bees
